            2. Runtime: The Number Manufacturers Are Least Honest About
          </h2>

          <p>
            Advertised runtime figures on cordless blowers are the least reliable specification in outdoor power equipment, and understanding why prevents most buying disappointment.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Advertised Runtime Assumes the Lowest Setting</h3>

          <p>
            A blower advertised at 90 minutes is almost always quoting runtime at its lowest speed with its largest available battery. That setting is useful for sweeping a dry patio and useless for moving leaves. At the setting you actually need for yard cleanup, expect a fraction of the quoted figure, often fifteen to twenty-five minutes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Power Draw Scales Steeply With Speed</h3>

          <p>
            Fan power consumption rises far faster than fan speed, which is why halving your speed setting can more than double runtime. This is the single most useful thing to know about operating a cordless blower: working at a moderate setting and reserving full power for stubborn spots dramatically extends how long you can work.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Battery Capacity Is the Other Half</h3>

          <p>
            Runtime scales directly with amp-hours, so a large pack runs proportionally longer than a compact one on the same tool. Compare watt-hours, voltage multiplied by amp-hours, when comparing across platforms, because amp-hour figures alone are not comparable between a 40V and an 80V system.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plan for Two Batteries</h3>

          <p>
            For any property with a real fall leaf drop, two batteries is the practical configuration. One works while the other charges, and a swap takes seconds. Two mid-size packs typically cost less than one very large pack and provide redundancy as the first ages.
