        <!-- SECTION 4: Reading Cordless Blower Specifications -->
        <div id="reading-specs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Reading Cordless Blower Specifications
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cordless blower marketing leans harder on impressive-sounding numbers than almost any other tool category. Here is what each figure actually tells you.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>CFM measures air volume.</strong> This is what moves a large mass of dry leaves forward efficiently, and it determines how fast a big cleanup goes. Above roughly 500 CFM a handheld blower stops feeling underpowered for lawn work.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>MPH measures air speed.</strong> This is what breaks stubborn material loose: wet leaves stuck to pavement, matted layers, pine needles wedged in gravel. Air speed matters more than volume when material is not moving at all, which is why a compact high-speed blower can outperform a larger high-volume one on wet leaves.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Peak figures versus sustained figures.</strong> Some manufacturers quote CFM and MPH at the nozzle under ideal conditions or in a momentary boost mode that cannot be sustained. Where a manufacturer publishes both a standard and a turbo figure, the standard one is the more meaningful number for judging everyday performance.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Newtons of blowing force.</strong> Where available, this combines volume and speed into a single measure of how hard the airstream pushes, and it is much harder to inflate through marketing than either number alone. Professional-grade equipment quotes it more often than consumer gear does.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Brushless is not optional.</strong> A brushless motor converts substantially more battery energy into airflow rather than waste heat, which directly improves both power and runtime. Any cordless blower without a brushless motor belongs to a previous generation regardless of when it was made.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">The Nozzle Changes the Numbers</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              A narrow nozzle raises air speed and lowers volume; a wide nozzle does the opposite. Manufacturers usually quote the figure that flatters the tool, so a blower advertising very high MPH may be quoting a narrow concentrator nozzle while quoting CFM with a wide one. If a blower ships with multiple nozzles, use the wide one for dry leaves on open ground and the narrow one for breaking loose wet or matted material.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Choosing a Battery Platform -->
        <div id="platforms" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Choosing a Battery Platform
          </h2>

          <p>
            A cordless blower is rarely the only battery tool you will own, and the platform decision outlives the tool by years. It is worth making deliberately rather than by accident.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Dedicated Outdoor Platforms</h3>

          <p>
            EGO 56V, Greenworks 80V, and Ryobi 40V are built specifically for outdoor equipment, covering mowers, blowers, trimmers, chainsaws, and snow throwers. They deliver the highest power for outdoor tasks, and their batteries are sized for the runtime yard work demands. The downside is that these packs do not run your drill.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Crossover Tool Platforms</h3>

          <p>
            Milwaukee M18, DeWalt FLEXVOLT, and Makita LXT are primarily jobsite platforms that also offer outdoor tools. If you already own drills and saws on one of these systems, adding a blower costs only the bare tool, which is a significant saving. The trade-off is that these packs are optimized for tools with intermittent draw rather than the sustained high current a blower demands, so runtime is often shorter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Consumer Crossover Platforms</h3>

          <p>
            Worx PowerShare and similar systems span a broad range of inexpensive tools at lower power levels. Good value for light-duty use and small properties, and generally not the right platform if you need to clear a heavy fall leaf drop.
          </p>

          <p>
            One factor worth weighing that rarely appears in comparisons: how easy it is to buy a replacement battery in eight years. Platforms sold through many retailers with a long track record are far safer bets than newer lines or ones tied to a single chain. A blower whose battery you cannot replace is a blower you throw away, regardless of how well the motor still works.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Practical Rule</h3>

          <p>
            If you already own batteries on any platform that makes a competent blower, buy that blower. The battery savings outweigh modest performance differences. If you are starting fresh and outdoor tools are your main use, choose a dedicated outdoor platform with the tool lineup you expect to want in five years.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Care, Storage, and Getting More Seasons Out of a Battery -->
        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Care, Storage, and Getting More Seasons Out of a Battery
          </h2>

          <p>
            The blower will almost certainly outlast its first battery, which makes battery care the most consequential maintenance you can do.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Store packs at partial charge in a temperate place.</strong> Lithium cells age fastest held at a high state of charge and in heat. Storing at roughly half charge somewhere indoors, rather than fully charged in a garage that swings from freezing to 100 degrees, measurably slows capacity loss.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Let hot packs cool before charging.</strong> A battery pulled off a blower after twenty minutes of full-power work is warm, and charging immediately adds more heat on top. Most quality chargers wait, but if yours starts immediately, give the pack fifteen minutes first.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Keep the air intake clear.</strong> Blowers pull a large volume of air through an intake screen, and leaves, grass, and dust accumulate there. A restricted intake reduces output and makes the motor work harder, which costs both performance and runtime. Check and clear it every few uses during heavy cleanup season.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Do not use a blower in the rain.</strong> Cordless blowers handle damp conditions and wet leaves fine, but running one in actual rain pulls water through the housing and past electronics that are not sealed against it. Wait for the rain to stop; wet leaves blow fine once it does.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Match the speed to the task.</strong> Beyond saving battery, running at moderate settings reduces wear on the motor, keeps noise down, and gives you far better control over where material goes. Full power is a tool for specific stubborn spots rather than a default operating mode.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Clean the housing after dusty work.</strong> Fine dust from a dry driveway or a gravel path works its way into vents and switch mechanisms. A quick wipe down and a blast of compressed air across the vents after dusty cleanups keeps the variable speed control and trigger operating smoothly, which is where grit tends to cause problems first.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Expect gradual decline rather than failure.</strong> Batteries do not stop working, they slowly hold less. Most people notice around year four or five that a cleanup that used to take one pack now takes two, at which point a fresh battery restores the tool to like-new performance for far less than a new blower.
          </p>
        </div>

