              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do cordless blowers run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Realistically 15 to 40 minutes depending on speed setting and battery capacity. Advertised figures are typically measured at the lowest speed with the largest battery, which is not the setting you use for leaves. Fan power draw rises steeply with speed, so halving your setting can more than double runtime.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can they handle wet leaves?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                High-voltage models at 600 CFM and above with air speeds around 180 MPH clear soaked matted leaves as effectively as gas handhelds. Air speed matters more than volume for wet material, so a blower advertising very high CFM but moderate MPH may push a wet mat around rather than lifting it. Prioritize MPH if your leaves are usually damp.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many batteries do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a property with a real fall leaf drop, two. One works while the other charges, and swapping takes seconds. Two mid-size packs typically cost less than one very large pack and give you redundancy as the first battery ages. For a small yard or driveway-only use, one large pack is usually enough.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are they as good as gas?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For handheld use, yes. Cordless blowers at 600 CFM and above match gas handhelds on clearing power while being far quieter and requiring no maintenance. Gas retains two advantages: unlimited runtime from a fuel can for multi-hour cleanups, and access to high-output backpack units above 900 CFM that have no cordless equivalent.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How loud are they?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Typically around 65 dB at the operator position, which is roughly conversation level and generally does not require hearing protection. Gas blowers commonly reach 95 dB. Because decibels are logarithmic, that gap represents a very large difference in perceived loudness and in how far the sound carries to neighbors.
              </p>
            </div>
