        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Cordless Blower Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cordless blower disappointment nearly always traces to runtime expectations or to buying on the wrong airflow specification.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Believing the Advertised Runtime</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Quoted runtime figures are measured at the lowest speed setting with the largest available battery, a combination almost nobody actually uses for leaf cleanup. At a working setting, expect a fraction of that number. Plan around 15 to 25 minutes at high power and buy a second battery if your cleanup takes longer.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying High CFM for Wet Leaves</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Air volume moves dry leaves efficiently but pushes a wet matted layer around rather than lifting it. Air speed is what breaks stuck material loose. If your leaves are typically damp, a blower with 180 MPH and moderate CFM will outperform one with 730 CFM and low air speed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running at Full Power Constantly</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fan power draw rises far faster than fan speed, so full throttle drains the battery disproportionately while also being louder and scattering the leaves you just gathered. Working at a moderate setting and reserving full power for stubborn spots more than doubles usable runtime.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Intake Screen</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Blowers pull large volumes of air through an intake that clogs with leaves, grass, and dust during heavy cleanup. A restricted intake reduces output noticeably and makes the motor work harder, costing runtime as well as power. Checking and clearing it every few uses takes seconds.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

